<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Heinrich Parler</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1300–c. 1370</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Heinrich Parler the Elder</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Heinrich Parler was a German master builder from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> who worked in Schwäbisch Gmünd, where from 1351 he built the choir of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-cross-minster">Holy Cross Minster</a>, one of the first late Gothic hall churches in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. He founded the Parler family of architects and sculptors; his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-parler">Peter Parler</a> built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-vitus-cathedral">St. Vitus Cathedral</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/heinrich-parler.jpg" alt="Heinrich Parler" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Beckstet at German Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gmuend_Muenster_von_Suedwest.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The family name comes from Parlier, meaning foreman. Heinrich Parler was probably born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, where his earliest known work was supervising construction at Cologne Cathedral, and he later lived and worked in Gmünd, an Imperial City of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. Around 1333 he was invited to become construction manager of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-cross-minster">Holy Cross Minster</a> there, where building had begun in 1315 to replace a Romanesque church with a Gothic basilica.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Parler amended the plan and completed the nave as a hall church, the first of its kind in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, with rounded pillars and leaf-wreath capitals, elongated tracery windows and complex coloured vaults. His style became more pronounced in the choir, whose foundation stone was laid in 1351 and on which his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-parler">Peter Parler</a> (1333–1399) worked beside him. Designed as an ambulatory with chapels set flat between the buttresses, it needed fewer internal columns and let the aisles rise as tall as the nave. He also made sculptures more lifelike than earlier churches had. Neither father nor son lived to see the consecration in 1410.</p><p>As was customary, he oversaw other building sites too. Though it is undocumented, he most likely designed the Frauenkirche in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> with Peter, its design strikingly close to Holy Cross, and the same comparison applies to the choir of Augsburg Cathedral, begun in 1356. He may also have had a hand in the design of Ulm Minster, begun in 1366, though members of his family are more clearly documented there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Many other masons took up and developed his architecture and sculpture, and the Parler style spread across central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, from St. Martin's Church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landshut">Landshut</a>, St. Lorenz in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a>, St. George's Minster in Dinkelsbühl and St. Stephen's Cathedral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> to churches of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and St. Mary's in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helsingor">Helsingør</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>. His eldest son, Johann Parler the Elder, worked at Zwettl and at Basel Cathedral. A brother may have been the foreman Peter von Reutlingen, presumed builder of the Marienkirche and the Nikolaikirche in Reutlingen.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Heinrich Parler” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Heinrich Parler</b> began the Holy Cross Minster there in the 14th century, one of the first German hall churches in the late Gothic style, and his son Peter Parler went on to build Prague Cathedral.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-schwabisch-gmund">Free Imperial City of Schwäbisch Gmünd</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Schwäbisch Gmünd, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.94,6.96&amp;year=1345" aria-label="Cologne on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Heinrich Parler"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5374 5752h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5374 5752h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.94,6.96&amp;year=1345">What was here in 1345? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
